window.QB_SUB = {"code":"B.3","title":"Gas laws","p1a":["22M.1A.SL.TZ2.10","22M.1A.SL.TZ2.11","22M.1A.SL.TZ1.12","19M.1A.SL.TZ2.14","19N.1A.SL.TZ0.11","19N.1A.SL.TZ0.10","19N.1A.SL.TZ0.9","21N.1A.SL.TZ0.10","19M.1A.SL.TZ2.12","21N.1A.SL.TZ0.12","19M.1A.SL.TZ2.13","20N.1A.SL.TZ0.7","19M.1A.SL.TZ1.13","22M.1A.SL.TZ2.12","20N.1A.SL.TZ0.11","20N.1A.SL.TZ0.9","21M.1A.SL.TZ1.10","21M.1A.SL.TZ1.25","21M.1A.SL.TZ1.12","21M.1A.SL.TZ2.11","20N.1A.SL.TZ0.12","21M.1A.SL.TZ2.10","22N.1A.SL.TZ0.10","22N.1A.SL.TZ0.11","SPM.1A.SL.TZ0.12","SPM.1A.HL.TZ0.17","EXE.1A.SL.TZ0.9","EXE.1A.SL.TZ0.10","EXE.1A.SL.TZ0.12","23M.1A.SL.TZ2.9","23M.1A.SL.TZ2.10","23M.1A.SL.TZ2.11","23M.1A.SL.TZ2.12"],"p2":[{"id":"19M.2.SL.TZ2.2","session":"19M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ2","num":2,"hits":["a","b","c","di","dii"],"marks":8,"parts":[{"label":"(a)","key":"a","subtopics":[],"pre":"<div class=\"specification\"> <p><span style=\"background-color: #ffffff;\">A container of volume 3.2 × 10-6 m<sup>3</sup> is filled with helium gas at a pressure of 5.1 × 10<sup>5</sup> Pa and temperature 320 K. Assume that this sample of helium gas behaves as an ideal gas.</span></p> </div><div class=\"specification\"> <p><span style=\"background-color: #ffffff;\">A helium atom has a volume of 4.9 × 10<sup>-31</sup> m<sup>3</sup>.</span></p> </div>","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">The molar mass of helium is 4.0 g mol<sup>-1</sup>. Show that the mass of a helium atom is 6.6 × 10<sup>-27</sup> kg.</span></p> <div class=\"marks\">[1]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><span class=\"mjpage\"><math alttext=\"m = \\frac{{4.0 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{6.02 \\times {{10}^{23}}}}\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi>m</mi> <mo>=</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>4.0</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>3</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>6.02</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>23</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mrow> </mfrac> </math></span>«kg»</span><em><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"></span></em></p> <p><em><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><strong>OR</strong></span></em></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">6.64 <span style=\"display:inline !important;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:left;text-decoration:none;text-indent:0px;white-space:normal;\">×</span> 10<span style=\"font-size:14px;\"><sup><span style=\"text-align:left;color:#000000;text-indent:0px;letter-spacing:normal;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;display:inline !important;white-space:normal;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;\">−27 </span></sup></span></span><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">«kg»</span><em><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"> ✔</span></em></p> <p> </p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>The mark was awarded for a clear substitution or an answer to at least 3sf. Many gained the mark for a clear substitution with a conversion from g to kg somewhere in their response. Fewer gave the answer to the correct number of sf.</p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(b)","key":"b","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">Estimate the average speed of the helium atoms in the container.</span></p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><span class=\"mjpage\"><math alttext=\"\\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} = \\frac{3}{2}kT/v = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3kT}}{m}} /\\sqrt {\\frac{{3 \\times 1.38 \\times {{10}^{ - 23}} \\times 320}}{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 27}}}}} \" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mn>1</mn> <mn>2</mn> </mfrac> <mi>m</mi> <mrow> <msup> <mi>v</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msup> </mrow> <mo>=</mo> <mfrac> <mn>3</mn> <mn>2</mn> </mfrac> <mi>k</mi> <mi>T</mi> <mrow> <mo>/</mo> </mrow> <mi>v</mi> <mo>=</mo> <msqrt> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>3</mn> <mi>k</mi> <mi>T</mi> </mrow> <mi>m</mi> </mfrac> </msqrt> <mrow> <mo>/</mo> </mrow> <msqrt> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>3</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mn>1.38</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>23</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> <mo>×</mo> <mn>320</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>6.6</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>27</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mrow> </mfrac> </msqrt> </math></span> <span style=\"display:inline !important;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:left;text-decoration:none;text-indent:0px;white-space:normal;\">✔</span></span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><em>v</em> = 1.4 <span style=\"display:inline !important;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:left;text-decoration:none;text-indent:0px;white-space:normal;\">×</span> 10<sup>3</sup> «<span style=\"display:inline !important;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:left;text-decoration:none;text-indent:0px;white-space:normal;\">ms</span><sup><span style=\"display:inline !important;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:left;text-decoration:none;text-indent:0px;white-space:normal;\">−</span><span style=\"display:inline !important;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:left;text-decoration:none;text-indent:0px;white-space:normal;\">1</span></sup>» ✔</span> </p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>At HL this was very well answered but at SL many just worked out E=3/2kT and left it as a value for KE.</p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(c)","key":"c","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">Show that the number of helium atoms in the container is about 4 × 10<sup>20</sup>.</span></p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">c.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><span class=\"mjpage\"><math alttext=\"N = \\frac{{pV}}{{kT}}/\\frac{{5.1 \\times {{10}^5} \\times 3.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}{{1.38 \\times {{10}^{ - 23}} \\times 320}}\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi>N</mi> <mo>=</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi>p</mi> <mi>V</mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mi>k</mi> <mi>T</mi> </mrow> </mfrac> <mrow> <mo>/</mo> </mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>5.1</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mn>5</mn> </msup> </mrow> <mo>×</mo> <mn>3.2</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>6</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>1.38</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>23</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> <mo>×</mo> <mn>320</mn> </mrow> </mfrac> </math></span></span></p> <p><em><strong>OR</strong></em></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><span class=\"mjpage\"><math alttext=\"N = \\frac{{pV{N_A}}}{{RT}}/\\frac{{5.1 \\times {{10}^5} \\times 3.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}} \\times 6.02 \\times {{10}^{23}}}}{{8.31 \\times 320}}\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi>N</mi> <mo>=</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi>p</mi> <mi>V</mi> <mrow> <msub> <mi>N</mi> <mi>A</mi> </msub> </mrow> </mrow> <mrow> <mi>R</mi> <mi>T</mi> </mrow> </mfrac> <mrow> <mo>/</mo> </mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>5.1</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mn>5</mn> </msup> </mrow> <mo>×</mo> <mn>3.2</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>6</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> <mo>×</mo> <mn>6.02</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>23</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>8.31</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mn>320</mn> </mrow> </mfrac> </math></span> <span style=\"display:inline !important;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:left;text-decoration:none;text-indent:0px;white-space:normal;\">✔</span></span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><em>N</em> = 3.7 <span style=\"display:inline !important;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:left;text-decoration:none;text-indent:0px;white-space:normal;\">× </span>10<sup>20</sup> ✔</span></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">c.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>Again at HL this was very well answered with the most common approach being to calculate the number of moles and then multiply by N<sub>A</sub> to calculate the number of atoms. At SL many candidates calculated n but stopped there. Also at SL there was some evidence of candidates working backwards and magically producing a value for ‘n’ that gave a result very close to that required after multiplying by N<sub>A</sub>.</p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">c.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(d)(i)","key":"di","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">Calculate the ratio <span class=\"mjpage\"><math alttext=\"\\frac{{{\\text{total volume of helium atoms}}}}{{{\\text{volume of helium gas}}}}\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mrow> <mtext>total volume of helium atoms</mtext> </mrow> </mrow> <mrow> <mrow> <mtext>volume of helium gas</mtext> </mrow> </mrow> </mfrac> </math></span>.</span></p> <div class=\"marks\">[1]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">di.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>«<span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><span class=\"mjpage\"><math alttext=\"\\frac{{4 \\times {{10}^{20}} \\times 4.9 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}}}}{{3.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}} =» 6 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>4</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>20</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> <mo>×</mo> <mn>4.9</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>31</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>3.2</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>6</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo>=</mo> <mrow> <mo>»</mo> </mrow> <mn>6</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mn>10</mn> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>5</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </math></span> ✔</span></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">di.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>This was well answered with the most common mistake being to use the volume of a single atom rather than the total volume of the atoms.</p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">di.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(d)(ii)","key":"dii","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">Explain, using your answer to (d)(i) and with reference to the kinetic model, why this sample of helium can be assumed to be an ideal gas.</span></p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">dii.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">«For an ideal gas» the size of the particles is small compared to the distance between them/size of the container/gas</span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><em><strong>OR</strong></em></span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">«For an ideal gas» the volume of the particles is negligible/the volume of the particles is small compared to the volume of the container/gas</span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><em><strong>OR</strong></em></span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">«For an ideal gas» particles are assumed to be point objects ✔</span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">calculation/ratio/result in (d)(i) shows that volume of helium atoms is negligible compared to/much smaller than volume of helium gas/container «hence assumption is justified» ✔</span></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">dii.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>In general this was poorly answered at SL. Many other non-related gas properties given such as no / negligible intermolecular forces, low pressure, high temperature. Some candidates interpreted the ratio as meaning it is a low density gas. At HL candidates seemed more able to focus on the key part feature of the question, which was the nature of the volumes involved. Examiners were looking for an assumption of the kinetic theory related to the volume of the atoms/gas and then a link to the ratio calculated in ci). The command terms were slightly different at SL and HL, giving slightly more guidance at SL.</p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">dii.</div> </div>"}],"aliases":[]},{"id":"21M.2.SL.TZ1.3","session":"21M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ1","num":3,"hits":["ai","aii"],"marks":12,"parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","key":"ai","subtopics":[],"pre":"<div class=\"specification\"> <p>A mass of 1.0 kg of water is brought to its boiling point of 100 °C using an electric heater of power 1.6 kW.</p> </div><div class=\"specification\"> <p>A mass of 0.86 kg of water remains after it has boiled for 200 s.</p> </div><div class=\"specification\"> <p>The electric heater has two identical resistors connected in parallel.</p> <p><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\"/></p> <p>The circuit transfers 1.6 kW when switch A only is closed. The external voltage is 220 V.</p> </div>","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>The molar mass of water is 18 g mol<sup>−1</sup>. Estimate the average speed of the water molecules in the vapor produced. Assume the vapor behaves as an ideal gas.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.i.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p><em>E</em><sub>k</sub> = « <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>38</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>373</mn><mo>)</mo></math>» = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow></msup></math> «J» <strong>✓</strong></p> <p><em>v = </em>«<em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>38</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>23</mn></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>373</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>018</mn></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></math></em>»<em> = </em>720 «m s<sup>−1</sup>» <strong>✓</strong></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.i.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.i.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(a)(ii)","key":"aii","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>State <strong>one</strong> assumption of the kinetic model of an ideal gas.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[1]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.ii.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>particles can be considered points «without dimensions» <strong>✓</strong></p> <p>no intermolecular forces/no forces between particles «except during collisions»<strong>✓</strong></p> <p>the volume of a particle is negligible compared to volume of gas <strong>✓</strong></p> <p>collisions between particles are elastic <strong>✓</strong></p> <p>time between particle collisions are greater than time of collision <strong>✓</strong></p> <p>no intermolecular PE/no PE between particles <strong>✓</strong></p> <p> </p> <p><em>Accept reference to atoms/molecules for “particle”</em></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.ii.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.ii.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(b)(i)","key":"bi","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>Estimate the specific latent heat of vaporization of water. State an appropriate unit for your answer.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.i.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>«<em>mL = P</em> t» so «<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1600</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>» = 2.3 x 10<sup>6</sup> «J kg<sup>-1</sup>» <strong>✓</strong></p> <p>J kg<sup>−1 </sup><strong>✓</strong></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.i.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.i.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(b)(ii)","key":"bii","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>Explain why the temperature of water remains at 100 °C during this time.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[1]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.ii.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>«all» of the energy added is used to increase the «intermolecular» potential energy of the particles/break «intermolecular» bonds/<strong>OWTTE</strong> <strong>✓</strong></p> <p><em>Accept reference to atoms/molecules for “particle”</em> </p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.ii.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.ii.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(c)","key":"c","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>The heater is removed and a mass of 0.30 kg of pasta at −10 °C is added to the boiling water.</p> <p>Determine the equilibrium temperature of the pasta and water after the pasta is added. Other heat transfers are negligible.</p> <p style=\"padding-left:180px;\">Specific heat capacity of pasta = 1.8 kJ kg<sup>−1</sup> K<sup>−1</sup><br/>Specific heat capacity of water = 4.2 kJ kg<sup>−1</sup> K<sup>−1</sup></p> <div class=\"marks\">[3]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">c.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>use of mcΔT <strong>✓</strong></p> <p>0.86 × 4200 × (100 – <em>T</em>) = 0.3 × 1800 × (<em>T</em> +10) <strong>✓</strong></p> <p><em>T</em><sub>eq</sub> = 85.69«°C» ≅ 86«°C» <strong>✓</strong></p> <p><em>Accept T<sub>eq</sub> in Kelvin (359 K).</em></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">c.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">c.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(d)(i)","key":"di","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>Show that each resistor has a resistance of about 30 Ω.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[1]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">d.i.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>R</mi></mfrac><mo> </mo><mi>so</mi><mo> </mo><mfrac><msup><mn>220</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>1600</mn></mfrac><mo> </mo><mi>so</mi><mo> </mo><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>25</mn></math> «Ω» <strong>✓</strong></p> <p><em>Must see either the substituted values <strong>OR</strong> a value for R to at least three s.f.</em></p> <p> </p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">d.i.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">d.i.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(d)(ii)","key":"dii","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>Calculate the power transferred by the heater when both switches are closed.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">d.ii.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>use of parallel resistors addition so <em>R</em><sub>eq</sub> = 15 «Ω» <strong>✓</strong></p> <p><em>P</em> = 3200 «W» <strong>✓</strong></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">d.ii.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">d.ii.</div> </div>"}],"aliases":[]},{"id":"22M.2.SL.TZ2.2","session":"22M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ2","num":2,"hits":["a","bi","bii"],"marks":6,"parts":[{"label":"(a)","key":"a","subtopics":[],"pre":"<div class=\"specification\"> <p>A fixed mass of an ideal gas is contained in a cylinder closed with a frictionless piston. The volume of the gas is 2.5 × 10<sup>−3 </sup>m<sup>3</sup> when the temperature of the gas is 37 °C and the pressure of the gas is 4.0 × 10<sup>5 </sup>Pa.</p> </div><div class=\"specification\"> <p>Energy is now supplied to the gas and the piston moves to allow the gas to expand. The temperature is held constant.</p> </div>","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>Calculate the number of gas particles in the cylinder.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>Correct conversion of T «T = 310 K» seen ✓</p> <p>« use of = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> to get » 2.3 × 10<sup>23</sup> ✓</p> <p> </p> <p><em>Allow <strong>ECF</strong> from <strong>MP1</strong> i.e., T in Celsius (Result is 2.7 x 10<sup>24</sup>)</em></p> <p><em>Allow use of n, R and N<sub>A</sub></em></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>a) This was well answered with the majority converting to K. Quite a few found the number of moles but did not then convert to molecules.</p> <p>bi) Well answered. It was pleasing to see how many recognised the need to state that the mass/number of molecules stayed the same as well as stating that the volume increased. At SL this recognition was less common so only 1 mark was often awarded.</p> <p>bii) This was less successfully answered. A surprising number of candidates said that the internal energy of an ideal gas increases during an isothermal expansion. Many recognised that constant temp meant constant KE but then went on to state that the PE must increase and so the internal energy would increase.</p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(b)(i)","key":"bi","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>Discuss, for this process, the changes that occur in the density of the gas.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.i.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>density decreases ✓</p> <p>volume is increased <em><strong>AND</strong> </em>mass/number of particles remains constant ✓</p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.i.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.i.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(b)(ii)","key":"bii","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>Discuss, for this process, the changes that occur in the internal energy of the gas.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.ii.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>internal energy is constant ✓</p> <p>internal energy depends on kinetic energy/temperature «only»</p> <p><em><strong>OR</strong></em></p> <p>since temperature/kinetic energy is constant ✓</p> <p> </p> <p><em>Do not award <strong>MP2</strong> for stating that “temperature is constant” unless linked to the correct conclusion, as that is mentioned in the stem.</em></p> <p><em>Award <strong>MP2</strong> for stating that kinetic energy remains constant.</em></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.ii.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.ii.</div> </div>"}],"aliases":[]},{"id":"22N.2.SL.TZ0.2","session":"22N","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":2,"hits":["bi","bii"],"marks":9,"parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","key":"ai","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qn_code_number\"> (a.i) </div> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Determine the minimum area of the solar heating panel required to increase the temperature of all the water in the tank to 30°C during a time of 1.0 hour. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> energy required = 250 × 4200 × (30 − 15) ✓ </p> <p> energy available = 0.30 × 680 × <em> t </em> × <em> A </em> ✓ </p> <p> <em> A </em> = « <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 250 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 4200 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 15 </mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn> 0 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 30 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 680 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 60 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 60 </mn> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo> = </mo> </math> » 21 «m <sup> 2 </sup> » <em> <strong> OR </strong> </em> 22 «m <sup> 2 </sup> » ✓ </p> <p> </p> <p> <em> Allow <strong> ECF </strong> from <strong> MP1 </strong> and <strong> MP2 </strong> . </em> </p> <p> <em> Accept the correct use of 0.30 in either <strong> MP1 </strong> or <strong> MP2 </strong> . </em> </p> </div>","er":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Most candidates had a good attempt at this but there were often slight slips. Some missed the efficiency of the process. Some included the albedo of the roof tiles. Some thought that the temperature rise needed to have 273 added to convert to kelvin. However, sometimes scoring through ECF (error carried forward), the average mark was around 2 marks. </p> </div>"},{"label":"(a)(ii)","key":"aii","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qn_code_number\"> (a.ii) </div> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Estimate, in °C, the temperature of the roof tiles. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> absorbed intensity = (1 − 0.2) × 680 «= 544» «W m <sup> −2 </sup> » </p> <p> <em> <strong> OR </strong> </em> </p> <p> emitted intensity = 0.97 × 5.67 × 10 <sup> −8 </sup> × T <sup> 4 </sup> ✓ </p> <p> </p> <p> <em> T </em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mo> = </mo> <mroot> <mfrac> <mn> 544 </mn> <mrow> <mn> 0 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 97 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 5 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 67 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 8 </mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mfrac> <mn> 4 </mn> </mroot> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 315 </mn> </math> «K» ✓ </p> <p> 42 «°C» ✓ </p> <p> </p> <p> <em> Allow <strong> ECF </strong> from <strong> MP1 </strong> and <strong> MP2 </strong> . </em> </p> <p> <em> Allow <strong> MP1 </strong> if absorbed or emitted intensity is multiplied by area. </em> </p> </div>","er":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> This was a bit more hit and miss than the previous question part. One common mistake was not understanding what albedo meant. Some took it as the amount of energy absorbed rather than reflected. Emissivity was often missed. Several candidates, successfully answering the question or not, were able to score MP3 converting the final temperature into Celsius degrees. </p> </div>"},{"label":"(b)(i)","key":"bi","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qn_code_number\"> (b.i) </div> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> State <strong> one </strong> way in which a real gas differs from an ideal gas. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> can be liquefied ✓ </p> <p> has intermolecular forces / potential energy ✓ </p> <p> has atoms/molecules that are not point objects / take up volume ✓ </p> <p> does not follow the ideal gas law «for all <em> T </em> and <em> p </em> » ✓ </p> <p> collisions between particles are non-elastic ✓ </p> <p> </p> <p> <em> Accept the converse argument. </em> </p> </div>","er":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> This was very well answered. Candidates showed an understanding of the differences between ideal and real gases. </p> </div>"},{"label":"(b)(ii)","key":"bii","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qn_code_number\"> (b.ii) </div> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> The water is heated. Explain why the quantity of air in the storage tank decreases. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <em> <strong> ALTERNATIVE 1 </strong> </em> </p> <p> «constant <em> p </em> and <em> V </em> imply» <em> nT </em> = const ✓ </p> <p> <em> T </em> increases hence <em> n </em> decreases ✓ </p> <p> <em> <strong> ALTERNATIVE 2 </strong> </em> </p> <p> «constant <em> p </em> and <em> n </em> imply» <em> V </em> is proportional to <em> T </em> / air expands as it is heated ✓ </p> <p> «original» air occupies a greater volume <strong> OR </strong> some air leaves through opening ✓ </p> <p> </p> <p> <em> <strong> MP2 </strong> in <strong> ALT 2 </strong> must come from expansion of air, not from expansion of water. </em> </p> <p> <em> Award <strong> [0] </strong> for an answer based on expansion of water. </em> </p> <p> <em> Award <strong> [1] </strong> <strong> max </strong> for an answer based on convection currents. </em> </p> </div>","er":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> It was surprising to see a large number of answers based on the expansion of water, as the stem of the question clearly states that the level of water remains constant. Most successful candidates scored by quoting <em> pV </em> constant so concluding with the inverse relationship of n and <em> T </em> , others also managed to score by explaining that the volume of air increases and therefore must go out through the opening. Answers based on convection currents were given partial credit. </p> </div>"}],"aliases":[]},{"id":"23M.2.SL.TZ2.2","session":"23M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ2","num":2,"hits":["a","b","c"],"marks":7,"parts":[{"label":"(a)","key":"a","subtopics":["B.3","T.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> State the unit for <em> pV </em> in fundamental SI units. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> kg m <sup> 2 </sup> s <sup> −2 </sup> ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)","key":"b","subtopics":["B.3","T.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Determine, using the graph, whether the gas acts as an ideal gas. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <em> <strong> ALTERNATIVE 1 </strong> </em> </p> <p> Graph shown is a straight line/linear <br/> <em> <strong> OR </strong> </em> <br/> expected graph should be a straight line/linear ✓ </p> <p> If ideal then <em> T </em> intercept must be at <em> T </em> = −273 °C ✓ </p> <p> Use of <em> y </em> = <em> mx </em> + <em> c </em> to show that <em> x </em> = −273 °C when <em> y </em> = 0 ✓ <br/> (hence ideal) </p> <p> <em> <strong> ALTERNATIVE 2 </strong> </em> </p> <p> Calculates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi> p </mi> <mi> V </mi> </mrow> <mi> T </mi> </mfrac> </math> for two different points ✓ <br/> Obtains 1.50 «J K <sup> −1 </sup> » for both ✓ </p> <p> States that for ideal gas <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi> p </mi> <mi> V </mi> </mrow> <mi> T </mi> </mfrac> <mo> = </mo> <mi> n </mi> <mi> R </mi> </math> which is constant and concludes that gas is ideal ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)","key":"c","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Calculate, in g, the mass of the gas. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Use of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> n </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi> p </mi> <mi> V </mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mi> R </mi> <mi> T </mi> </mrow> </mfrac> </math> <em> <strong> OR </strong> </em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> N </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi> p </mi> <mi> V </mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mi> k </mi> <mi> T </mi> </mrow> </mfrac> </math> ✓ </p> <p> Mass of gas = <em> n </em> × <em> N </em> <sub> A </sub> × mass of molecule <br/> <em> <strong> OR </strong> </em> <br/> Mass of gas = N <em> </em> × mass of molecule ✓ </p> <p> 5.1 «g» ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"}],"aliases":[]},{"id":"EXE.2.HL.TZ0.1","session":"EXE","level":"HL","tz":"TZ0","num":1,"hits":["cii"],"marks":20,"parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","key":"ai","subtopics":["E.4"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> State the principal energy change in nuclear fission. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Mass-energy «of uranium» into kinetic energy of fission products ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(a)(ii)","key":"aii","subtopics":["E.4"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> The energy released in the reaction is about 180 MeV. Estimate, in J, the energy released when 1 kg of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mmultiscripts> <mtext> U </mtext> <mprescripts> </mprescripts> <mn> 92 </mn> <mn> 235 </mn> </mmultiscripts> </math> undergoes fission. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Mass of uranium nucleus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mo> ≈ </mo> <mn> 235 </mn> <mtext> u </mtext> </math> ✓ </p> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mtext> energy </mtext> <mtext> mass </mtext> </mfrac> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 180 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mn> 6 </mn> </msup> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 1 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 60 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 19 </mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> <mrow> <mn> 235 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 1 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 661 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 27 </mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mfrac> </math> ✓ </p> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mn> 7 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 4 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mn> 13 </mn> </msup> </math> «J» ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)(i)","key":"bi","subtopics":["E.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> State and explain the decay mode of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mmultiscripts> <mtext> Te </mtext> <mprescripts> </mprescripts> <mn> 52 </mn> <mn> 132 </mn> </mmultiscripts> </math> . </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> beta minus decay ✓ </p> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mmultiscripts> <mtext> Te </mtext> <mprescripts> </mprescripts> <mn> 52 </mn> <mn> 132 </mn> </mmultiscripts> </math> has more neutrons / higher <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mi> N </mi> <mi> Z </mi> </mfrac> </math> ratio than stable nuclides of similar <em> A </em> «and beta minus reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mi> N </mi> <mi> Z </mi> </mfrac> </math> » ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)(ii)","key":"bii","subtopics":["E.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Calculate, in s <sup> −1 </sup> , the initial activity of the sample. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <msup> <mi> e </mi> <mn> 25 </mn> </msup> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 7 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 2 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mn> 10 </mn> </msup> </math> «s <sup> −1 </sup> » ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)(iii)","key":"biii","subtopics":["E.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Show that the decay constant of a nuclide is given by − <em> m </em> , where <em> m </em> is the slope of the graph of ln <em> A </em> against <em> t </em> . </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Takes ln of both sides of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> A </mi> <mo> = </mo> <msub> <mi> A </mi> <mn> 0 </mn> </msub> <msup> <mi> e </mi> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mi> λ </mi> <mi> t </mi> </mrow> </msup> </math> , leading to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> ln </mi> <mo> </mo> <mi> A </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mi> ln </mi> <mo> </mo> <msub> <mi> A </mi> <mn> 0 </mn> </msub> <mo> - </mo> <mi> λ </mi> <mi> t </mi> </math> ✓ </p> <p> «hence slope <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mo> = </mo> <mo> - </mo> <mi> λ </mi> </math> » ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)(iv)","key":"biv","subtopics":["E.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Determine, in days, the half-life of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mmultiscripts> <mtext> Te </mtext> <mprescripts> </mprescripts> <mn> 52 </mn> <mn> 132 </mn> </mmultiscripts> </math> . </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Slope =«−» <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mn> 2 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 5 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 6 </mn> </mrow> </msup> </math> «s <sup> −1 </sup> » ✓ </p> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <msub> <mi> T </mi> <mfrac> <mn> 1 </mn> <mn> 2 </mn> </mfrac> </msub> <mo> = </mo> </math> « <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi> ln </mi> <mo> </mo> <mn> 2 </mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn> 2 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 5 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 6 </mn> </mrow> </msup> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 60 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 60 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 24 </mn> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo> = </mo> </math> » 3.2 «days» ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)(i)","key":"ci","subtopics":["E.4"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Outline the role of the heat exchanger in a nuclear power station. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Collects thermal energy from the coolant and delivers it to the gas ✓ </p> <p> Prevents the «irradiated» coolant from leaving the reactor vessel ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)(ii)","key":"cii","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Calculate the maximum temperature of the gas during the cycle. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Correct read offs of pressure and volume at B ✓ </p> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mn> 8 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 0 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mn> 6 </mn> </msup> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 1 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 6 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 3 </mn> </mrow> </msup> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 1 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 0 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 8 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 31 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mi> T </mi> </math> ✓ </p> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> T </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 1500 </mn> </math> «K» ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)(iii)","key":"ciii","subtopics":["B.4"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Outline why the entropy of the gas remains constant during changes BC and DA. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mtext> Δ </mtext> <mi> S </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mtext> Δ </mtext> <mi> Q </mi> </mrow> <mi> T </mi> </mfrac> </math> , the change in entropy is zero when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mtext> Δ </mtext> <mi> Q </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 0 </mn> </math> ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)(iv)","key":"civ","subtopics":["B.4"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Determine the efficiency of the cycle. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Net work done = « <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mn> 8 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 23 </mn> <mo> + </mo> <mn> 9 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 11 </mn> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 4 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 32 </mn> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 3 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 25 </mn> </math> » 9.77 «kJ» ✓ </p> <p> Efficiency =« <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 9 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 77 </mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn> 20 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 58 </mn> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo> = </mo> </math> » 0.47 ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(d)","key":"d","subtopics":["A.4"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Show that the rotational kinetic energy of the turbine decreases at a constant rate. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Rotational KE is proportional to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <msup> <mi> ω </mi> <mn> 2 </mn> </msup> </math> ✓ </p> <p> Calculation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <msup> <mi> ω </mi> <mn> 2 </mn> </msup> </math> for at least four points, e.g. {96.1, 76.7, 57.6, 38.4, 19.3}×10 <sup> 3 </sup> </p> <p> Shows that the differences in equal time intervals are approximately the same, e.g. {19.4, 19.1, 19.2, 19.1, 19.3}×10 <sup> 3 </sup> ✓ </p> <p> </p> <p> <em> Allow a tolerance of ±1×10 <sup> 3 </sup> s <sup> −2 </sup> from the values stated in MP2. </em> </p> </div>","er":"None"}],"aliases":[]},{"id":"EXE.2.SL.TZ0.7","session":"EXE","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":7,"hits":["a","b","c"],"marks":7,"parts":[{"label":"(a)","key":"a","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> State <strong> two </strong> assumptions of the kinetic model of an ideal gas that refer to intermolecular collisions. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> The collisions are elastic ✓ </p> <p> The time for a collision is much shorter than the time between collisions ✓ </p> <p> The intermolecular forces are only present during collisions ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)","key":"b","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Discuss how the motion of the molecules of a gas gives rise to pressure in the gas. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> The momentum of a molecule changes when it collides with a container wall ✓ </p> <p> From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> F </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mtext> Δ </mtext> <mi> p </mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext> Δ </mtext> <mi> t </mi> </mrow> </mfrac> </math> and Newton’s third law, this leads to a force exerted on the wall by the molecule ✓ </p> <p> The average force exerted by all the molecules on a unit area of the wall is equivalent to pressure ✓ </p> <p> </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)","key":"c","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> The average speed of the molecules of a gas is 500 m s <sup> −1 </sup> . The density of the gas is 1.2 kg m <sup> −3 </sup> . Calculate, in kPa, the pressure of the gas. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mn> 1 </mn> <mn> 3 </mn> </mfrac> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 1 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 2 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 500 </mn> <mn> 2 </mn> </msup> </math> ✓ </p> <p> 100 «kPa» ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"}],"aliases":[]},{"id":"EXE.2.SL.TZ0.8","session":"EXE","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":8,"hits":["a","b","c"],"marks":7,"parts":[{"label":"(a)","key":"a","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Calculate the average translational speed of air molecules. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mn> 1 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 8 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mn> 5 </mn> </msup> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mn> 1 </mn> <mn> 3 </mn> </mfrac> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 2 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 0 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mi> v </mi> <mn> 2 </mn> </msup> </math> ✓ </p> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> v </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 520 </mn> </math> «m s <sup> −1 </sup> » ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)","key":"b","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> The air is a mixture of nitrogen, oxygen and other gases. Explain why the component gases of air in the container have different average translational speeds. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Average kinetic energy of the molecules is determined by the temperature only ✓ </p> <p> The mass of a molecule is different for each component gas ✓ </p> <p> From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <msub> <mi> E </mi> <mtext> k </mtext> </msub> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mn> 1 </mn> <mn> 2 </mn> </mfrac> <mi> m </mi> <msup> <mi> v </mi> <mn> 2 </mn> </msup> </math> , the same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <msub> <mi> E </mi> <mtext> k </mtext> </msub> </math> and different mass implies a different average velocity ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)","key":"c","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> The temperature of the sample is increased without a change in pressure. Outline the effect it has on the density of the gas. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <strong> <em> ALTERNATIVE 1 </em> </strong> </p> <p> The average translational speed increases «because <em> T </em> increases» ✓ </p> <p> From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> P </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mn> 1 </mn> <mn> 3 </mn> </mfrac> <mi> ρ </mi> <msup> <mi> v </mi> <mn> 2 </mn> </msup> </math> , the density decreases «to keep <em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> P </mi> </math> </em> constant» ✓ </p> <p> <br/> <strong> <em> ALTERNATIVE 2 </em> </strong> </p> <p> From the ideal gas law, the volume of the gas increases ✓ </p> <p> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> ρ </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mi> m </mi> <mi> v </mi> </mfrac> </math> and <em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> m </mi> </math> </em> is constant, the density decreases ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"}],"aliases":[]},{"id":"EXE.2.SL.TZ0.9","session":"EXE","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":9,"hits":["ai","aii","b","c"],"marks":8,"parts":[{"label":"(a)","key":"a","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Outline how the concept of absolute zero of temperature is interpreted in terms of: </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> it is the temperature at which the volume </p> <p> <strong> <em> OR </em> </strong> </p> <p> the pressure extrapolates to zero (can be shown by sketch) ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(a)(i)","key":"ai","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> the ideal gas law, </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> it is the temperature at which the volume </p> <p> <strong> <em> OR </em> </strong> </p> <p> the pressure extrapolates to zero (can be shown by sketch) ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(a)(ii)","key":"aii","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> the kinetic energy of particles in an ideal gas. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> it is the temperature at which all the (random) motion stops </p> <p> <strong> <em> OR </em> </strong> </p> <p> at which all the motion can be extrapolated to stop </p> <p> <strong> <em> OR </em> </strong> </p> <p> at which the kinetic energy of all particles is zero ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)","key":"b","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> A container holds a mixture of argon and helium atoms at a temperature of 37 °C. </p> <p> Calculate the average translational speed of the argon atoms. </p> <p> The molar mass of argon is 4.0 × 10 <sup> −2 </sup> kg mol <sup> −1 </sup> . </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [4] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Use of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 4 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 0 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 2 </mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> <msub> <mi> N </mi> <mtext> A </mtext> </msub> </mfrac> <mfenced> <mrow> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 6 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 6 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 26 </mn> </mrow> </msup> <mo> </mo> <mtext> kg </mtext> </mrow> </mfenced> </math> ✓ </p> <p> Work showing that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> v </mi> <mfenced> <mrow> <mo> = </mo> <msqrt> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 3 </mn> <mi> p </mi> </mrow> <mi> ρ </mi> </mfrac> <mo> = </mo> <msqrt> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 3 </mn> <mi> p </mi> <mi> V </mi> </mrow> <mi> m </mi> </mfrac> </msqrt> </msqrt> </mrow> </mfenced> <mo> = </mo> <msqrt> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 3 </mn> <mi> k </mi> <mi> T </mi> </mrow> <mi> m </mi> </mfrac> </msqrt> </math> ✓ </p> <p> Correct substitution AND conversion to K (310 K) ✓ </p> <p> 430/440 «m s <sup> −1 </sup> » ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)","key":"c","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Discuss how the mean kinetic energy of the argon atoms in the mixture compares with that of the helium atoms. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> the gases are in the same container at the same temperature so are <span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"> in equilibrium </span> ✓ </p> <p> they must have the same mean/average kinetic energy ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"}],"aliases":[]},{"id":"EXE.2.SL.TZ0.10","session":"EXE","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":10,"hits":["a","b","c","d"],"marks":7,"parts":[{"label":"(a)","key":"a","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Calculate the pressure of the gas in the container. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> p </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi> n </mi> <mi> R </mi> <mi> T </mi> </mrow> <mi> V </mi> </mfrac> </math> seen <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 12 </mn> <mo> </mo> <mtext> kPa </mtext> </math> ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)","key":"b","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Determine the mass of the gas in the container. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mn> 6 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 4 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 2 </mn> </mrow> </msup> <mo> </mo> <mtext> kg </mtext> </math> ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)","key":"c","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Calculate the average translational speed of the gas particles. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> v </mi> <mo> = </mo> <msqrt> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 3 </mn> <mi> P </mi> <mi> V </mi> </mrow> <mi> M </mi> </mfrac> </msqrt> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 450 </mn> <mo> </mo> <msup> <mtext> m s </mtext> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 1 </mn> </mrow> </msup> </math> ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(d)","key":"d","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> The temperature of the gas in the container is increased. </p> <p> Explain, using the kinetic theory, how this change leads to a change in pressure in the container. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [4] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> increased temperature means increased average KE and hence increased average translational speed ✓ </p> <p> This increases the momentum transfer at the walls for each collision / <em> mv </em> is greater per collision ✓ </p> <p> This increases the frequency of collisions at the walls / particles cover the distance between walls more quickly ✓ </p> <p> Ideas that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <msub> <mi> F </mi> <mtext> wall </mtext> </msub> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mtext> Δ </mtext> <mi> p </mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext> Δ </mtext> <mi> t </mi> </mrow> </mfrac> </math> AND <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> P </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mi> F </mi> <mi> A </mi> </mfrac> </math> (can be in words) so that force increases and pressure <span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"> increases </span> ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"}],"aliases":[]}]};
